ProsperoSunshine: /launch says which title is open when the host doesn't start the game a client picked

The PS5 host doesn't start a game while another title is open on the console, because starting one would close the other
and could lose its progress (process_ps5.cpp, start_title). Moonlight then only showed Sunshine's "Failed to start the
specified application". Now the launch reply carries the reason process_ps5.cpp gives: which title is open (unless the
device may not see it) and to close it first or pick PS5 Screen.

--- a/src/process.h
+++ b/src/process.h
@@ -273,5 +273,11 @@
    * @brief app_allowed() for an app ID, as /launch and /resume get it.
    */
   bool app_id_allowed(const std::string &client_cert, int app_id);
+
+  /**
+   * @brief Why the last execute() on this thread didn't start its title, for Moonlight to show (process_ps5.cpp); empty when
+   * it gave no reason. Taking it clears it.
+   */
+  std::string take_launch_refusal();
 #endif
 }  // namespace proc
--- a/src/nvhttp.cpp
+++ b/src/nvhttp.cpp
@@ -1699,7 +1699,13 @@
       auto err = proc::proc.execute((int) appid, launch_session);
       if (err) {
         tree.put("root.<xmlattr>.status_code", err);
+#ifdef __SCE__
+        // Another title open on the console stays open: Moonlight says which one and what to do (process_ps5.cpp).
+        const auto refusal = proc::take_launch_refusal();
+        tree.put("root.<xmlattr>.status_message", refusal.empty() ? std::string("Failed to start the specified application") : refusal);
+#else
         tree.put("root.<xmlattr>.status_message", "Failed to start the specified application");
+#endif
         tree.put("root.gamesession", 0);
 
         return;
